WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.560
We’re trying to measure gravity between
two microscopic objects — so micron-sized

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.240
objects, one-millionth of a meter in size,
so about one-one-hundredth the width of a

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.409
human hair. And we do this by actually levitating
a glass sphere that’s five microns in diameter

00:00:14.409 --> 00:00:19.210
in a high vacuum chamber. And we levitate
it with a laser, so actually the laser, the

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.460
photons or the light in the laser, pushes
on the microsphere or this five-micron glass

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.509
sphere hard enough to hold it up against gravity
and float it in the center of our vacuum chamber.

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:32.160
And because it’s then sitting in our vacuum
chamber, we can decouple it from all the forces

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.160
that you would get if you were in the environment
and you had air pushing against you and other

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.870
things that could degrade the ability to see
these very tiny forces from gravitational

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.350
interactions or gravity-like interactions
that we’re looking for. So to do this we

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:50.430
need lasers. We need to be able to have an
optical table where we can take a laser, focus

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.700
it inside of our vacuum chamber. Once we bring
the light to a focus, then you get a very

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.600
intense laser light which is strong enough
to hold up this microsphere against gravity.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.090
We then actually drop these microspheres in-vacuum.
So we smear them on to the bottom of a glass

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.470
cover slip. We shake that cover slip very
hard and some of these microspheres fall off

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.760
and they fall under gravity. And a very tiny
fraction of them will actually fall through

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.030
this micron-sized focus of our laser. So the
analogy might be that it’s like dropping

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.870
a basketball of the Empire State Building
and expecting it to fall through the hoop

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:28.640
on the ground floor. So this happens very
rarely, but obviously we can drop very many

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.840
of these microspheres and some of them will
fall through that hoop and become trapped.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.340
Once they’re trapped, then they’re very
well decoupled from the environment and we

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.310
can see these extremely tiny forces that we
want to see interacting on them. So we bring

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.420
a dense test mass nearby, so a gold test mass
or a very dense material that’s also microns

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.260
in size to micron separations and then we
look for some attractive force as we start

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.909
to get that test mass very near the microsphere
that’s held in our laser trap.

